# Hands-on Lab 2 – DNS Investigation

## Objective

Use Windows networking tools to investigate how DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.

### Commands Used

```cmd
ipconfig /all
nslookup github.com
nslookup google.com
nslookup microsoft.com
nslookup openai.com
```

---

## DNS Configuration

| Item | Value |
|------|-------|
| IPv4 Address | 192.168.0.2 |
| Default Gateway | 192.168.0.1 |
| DNS Server | 192.168.0.1 |
| DHCP Enabled | Yes |

### Observation

My computer uses **192.168.0.1** (my home router) as its DNS server. The router forwards DNS requests to my Internet Service Provider or another configured upstream DNS service.

---

# DNS Lookup Results

| Domain | Result |
|---------|--------|
| github.com | 140.82.121.4 |
| google.com | 172.217.22.78 (IPv4), 2a00:1450:4007:817::200e (IPv6) |
| microsoft.com | DNS request timed out |
| openai.com | 172.64.154.211, 104.18.33.45 |

---

## Analysis

### GitHub

GitHub successfully resolved to a single IPv4 address, demonstrating how DNS converts domain names into IP addresses.

### Google

Google returned both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, showing support for dual-stack networking.

### OpenAI

OpenAI returned multiple IPv4 addresses, indicating load balancing and redundant infrastructure commonly used by large cloud services.

### Microsoft

The Microsoft lookup timed out. Since other domains resolved successfully, this likely indicates a temporary DNS resolution issue or connectivity delay rather than a security incident.

