# Why DNS Matters in Cybersecurity

DNS activity provides valuable evidence during investigations.

SOC analysts review DNS logs to:

- Identify malicious domain lookups
- Detect Command-and-Control (C2) communication
- Investigate malware infections
- Discover phishing domains
- Monitor unusual outbound traffic
- Support incident response investigations

---

# Key Takeaways

- Firewalls are the first layer of network defense.
- IDS detects threats, while IPS detects and blocks threats.
- Network segmentation reduces lateral movement during attacks.
- SSH (Port 22) requires strong security controls because it provides remote administrative access.
- DNS converts domain names into IP addresses.
- DNS logs are an essential data source during SOC investigations.
- Multiple IP addresses for large websites are normal and often indicate load balancing and redundancy.

